## Refactored with Async/Await

Here's the refactored version with several improvements:

```javascript
async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);

  if (!rows.length) {
    throw new Error(`User with id "${id}" not found`);
  }

  return rows[0];
}
```

### Usage Example

```javascript
// Basic usage
try {
  const user = await getUser(1);
  console.log(user);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Failed to get user:', err.message);
}

// Or with .then()/.catch() if not in an async context
getUser(1)
  .then(user => console.log(user))
  .catch(err => console.error(err.message));
```

---

### What Changed and Why

| Before | After | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `callback(err)` | `throw new Error(...)` | Async functions reject the returned Promise on throw |
| `callback(null, rows[0])` | `return rows[0]` | Return value becomes the Promise's resolved value |
| `callback(new Error(...))` | `throw new Error(...)` | Consistent error handling via try/catch |
| Generic `'Not found'` message | `User with id "${id}" not found` | More descriptive error message |

---

### Important Caveat

This assumes `db.query()` **already returns a Promise**. If it's still callback-based, wrap it first:

```javascript
// Wrap once, reuse everywhere
const query = (sql, params) =>
  new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    db.query(sql, params, (err, rows) => {
      if (err) reject(err);
      else resolve(rows);
    });
  });

// Then use it cleanly
async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);

  if (!rows.length) {
    throw new Error(`User with id "${id}" not found`);
  }

  return rows[0];
}
```

> **Tip:** Many modern database libraries (like `mysql2/promise`, `pg`, `knex`) expose Promise-based APIs natively — prefer those over manual wrapping.
